2026-04-02 Update - Startup Blocker Identified:
- Re-ran the `start.ai` bootstrap checks at session start.
- Confirmed `specify` is installed and runnable via `specify`.
- Confirmed the current branch is `main`.
- Confirmed there is still no monthly vulnerability audit entry recorded within the last 30 days, so the audit remains due once startup prerequisites are satisfied.
- Identified a bootstrap blocker: `.repo_ai` is not attached as a real git submodule in the parent repository.
- Evidence:
- `git submodule status` returned no submodules.
- Root `.gitmodules` is missing.
- `git ls-files --stage .repo_ai .repo_ai/repo.ai` shows regular tracked files (`100644`) instead of a submodule gitlink (`160000`).
- `.repo_ai/.git` points to `../.git/modules/.repo_ai`, but that gitdir does not exist, so `.repo_ai` is currently a broken vendored copy rather than a working submodule checkout.
- Per `start.ai`, further execution is paused until `.repo_ai` is repaired as a submodule.

2026-04-02 Update - `.repo_ai` Submodule Repair:
- Removed the broken tracked-copy form of `.repo_ai` from the index and re-added it as a proper git submodule from `https://github.com/fishdan/repo-ai.git`.
- `git submodule status` now reports `.repo_ai` at commit `26312e34921d1fcce1fce4d20c675b6c6b1a1c3c` on `heads/main`.
- This satisfies the `start.ai` prerequisite that `.repo_ai` be attached as a submodule.
- Spec Kit task note: the submodule-repair startup task can be considered complete.

